# 2. Vahanwala's uniform construction is actually doubly stochastic

Vahanwala uses the column-stochastic convention.

Let the number of Markov states be

\[
n=k+1.
\]

Choose

\[
s=\frac1n\mathbf1,
\]

so

\[
S=s\mathbf1^\top
=
J
=
\frac1n\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top.
\]

His construction imposes

\[
SD=0,
\qquad
DS=0.
\]

Since

\[
SD
=
\frac1n\mathbf1(\mathbf1^\top D),
\]

we obtain

\[
\mathbf1^\top D=0.
\]

Since

\[
DS
=
\frac1n(D\mathbf1)\mathbf1^\top,
\]

we obtain

\[
D\mathbf1=0.
\]

Thus \(D\) has both zero column sums and zero row sums.

Therefore

\[
M=J+D
\]

has both row sums and column sums equal to \(1\).

All entries are chosen strictly positive in the reduction.

### Theorem CW-002J.1 — Doubly-stochastic strengthening of the Vahanwala reduction

The published reduction may be instantiated so that the resulting \((k+1)\)-state Markov chain is

\[
\boxed{
\text{strictly positive and doubly stochastic}.
}
\]

Its stationary distribution is exactly uniform.

This conclusion follows directly from the equations in the published construction; the paper states ergodicity rather than emphasizing bistochasticity.

