# 3. Stationary-coordinate deviations live in an \((n-1)\)-dimensional tangent space

Let \(M\) be any \(n\times n\) doubly stochastic matrix.

Set

\[
J=\frac1n\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top,
\qquad
D=M-J.
\]

Then

\[
D\mathbf1=0,
\qquad
\mathbf1^\top D=0.
\]

Therefore the zero-sum subspace

\[
\boxed{
H
=
\{x\in\mathbb Q^n:\mathbf1^\top x=0\}
}
\]

is \(D\)-invariant and has dimension

\[
\dim H=n-1.
\]

Also

\[
JD=DJ=0,
\]

so for every \(t\ge1\),

\[
\boxed{
M^t=J+D^t.
}
\]

For coordinates \(i,j\),

\[
(M^t)_{ij}-\frac1n
=
e_i^\top D^t e_j.
\]

Since \(De_j\in H\), for \(t\ge1\) the nonstationary sequence is generated entirely by the restriction

\[
D_H:=D|_H,
\]

an \((n-1)\)-dimensional rational linear operator.

By Cayley–Hamilton:

### Theorem CW-002J.2 — Tangent-order bound

For every \(n\)-state rational doubly stochastic Markov chain and every pair \(i,j\), the stationary-coordinate deviation sequence

\[
\boxed{
a_t
=
(M^t)_{ij}-\frac1n,
\qquad t\ge1,
}
\]

is a rational linear recurrence sequence of order at most

\[
\boxed{n-1}.
\]

The stationary eigenvalue \(1\) has disappeared exactly; it does not consume an LRS dimension.

