# 4. Dimension-tight reduction in the other direction

Vahanwala starts from an arbitrary rational order-\(k\) LRS

\[
u_0,u_1,\ldots
\]

and constructs a positive \((k+1)\)-state chain satisfying

\[
(M^t)_{ij}
=
\frac1{k+1}
+
\frac{\eta}{\rho^t}u_t
\]

for positive \(\eta,\rho\).

Hence

\[
\operatorname{sgn}
\left(
(M^t)_{ij}-\frac1{k+1}
\right)
=
\operatorname{sgn}(u_t).
\]

Combining with CW-002J.1:

### Theorem CW-002J.3 — Stationary-cutpoint / LRS dictionary

Up to complementing the inequality, the following \(n\)-state problem is polynomial-time interreducible, with the natural dimension shift, with Positivity for LRS of order at most \(n-1\):

> Input: a rational strictly-positive doubly-stochastic \(n\times n\) matrix \(M\), states \(i,j\).  
> Question:
> \[
> \exists t\ge1:
> (M^t)_{ij}<\frac1n?
> \]

The upper direction is CW-002J.2 plus Cayley–Hamilton.

The lower direction is Vahanwala's \(k\mapsto k+1\) reduction with the doubly-stochastic observation above.

Thus the exact stationary threshold

\[
\boxed{\frac1n}
\]

is not an arbitrary numerical nuisance.

It is the coordinate expression of the tangent-space LRS.

