# 8. Kannan–Lipton target-geometry inversion

Kannan and Lipton's classical Orbit Problem asks:

\[
\exists t:
A^t x=y?
\]

for rational \(A,x,y\).

They proved this point-target problem decidable in polynomial time.

Now compare the same one-generator setting with an apparently simpler target:

\[
\boxed{
c^\top A^t x<\lambda?
}
\]

a halfspace.

For a single positive doubly-stochastic Markov matrix at the stationary threshold, this halfspace question is Positivity-equivalent and unresolved in general.

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{point target}
\quad\text{can be algorithmically easier than}\quad
\text{halfspace target}.
}
\]

This is not a paradox.

Point hitting asks an exact orbit-membership equation amenable to algebraic spectral methods.

Halfspace hitting asks for the sign pattern of an exponential-polynomial sequence, where Diophantine approximation enters.

The difficulty lies in asymptotic sign geometry, not target-set dimension alone.

